														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2337761">
									

<p>I want to like this concept, but&#8230;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2362 will remain known to scientists and astronomy enthusiasts, but common knowledge of SCP-2362 will likely subside within one to two generations as it is no longer included in textbook lists of planets and receives less attention from educational programming.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not really sure why scientists and astronomy buffs are excluded. How can they be guaranteed to not let loose the truth? Furthermore, just because something is no longer taught in schools, it doesn't mean that the bit of information is lost to the general populace. One can simply Google quite a bit about Pluto nonetheless. Especially in an Internet-driven age, the memory of Pluto would need to be outright erased to safeguard secrecy.</p>
<p>And I feel the implication that similar planetoids like Eris are similar to this anomalous Pluto to be slightly cheap and the overall effect is somewhat underwhelming.</p>
<p>Overall&#8230; let's give a neutral for now&#8230;</p>

							</div>

								
