														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2337884">
									

<blockquote>
<p>■Keter class SCPs require extremely specific containment procedures, and are simply so difficult to contain effectively that they need higher levels of care and resources to suppress. If you lock it in a box, and there's a chance it bursts out through the lid of its own accord unless you follow very stringent containment procedures, it's Keter. A Keter class SCP needs to stand out from other articles and be a unique threat all its own. This class includes Shadow Person, An Incomplete Chronicle, and The Maybe There Monsters.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, but being difficult to contain is only part of it. It doesn't pose a unique threat. SCP-1548 (a celestial body as well) does both. It is difficult to contain (prevent knowledge to population) and poses a unique threat (oh shit, it's coming to get us). This SCP is only half that. That's why I stated that it needs more to be Keter. That's why I suggested the asteroid belt. Hell, make a moon of Mars an instance with indication that it may hatch soon. Use a model of the Pluto instance's exit path to show a possible collision course with Earth. There is so much that can be done to make it really feel Keter, but it just doesn't do it for me.</p>

