			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2337941">
									

<p>By that logic, <a href="/scp-2557">SCP-2557</a> shouldn't be Keter either. Potential information breaches regarding the Foundation or the existence of the anomalous are just as important to contain because even if the object itself doesn't have devastating implications on humanity, public knowledge of things that make the laws of nature look like a bad joke very well might. People see a space jellyfish, and they start looking harder. Soon they find a <a href="/scp-2460">mass of dark matter orbiting Earth</a>, a <a href="/scp-1812">memetic second moon</a>, and then the whole charade falls apart. The Foundation works to preserve normalcy, as anything else is inimical to the existence of humanity as we know it (whether or not that's always the most noble goal).</p>

